<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Til Barsip</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell Ahmar, Kar-Shalmaneser, Masuwari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Til Barsip, at Tell Ahmar on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, was an ancient city that became the capital of the Aramaean state of Bit-Adini. Earlier it was the Luwian city of Masuwari. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> took it in 856 BCE, renamed it Kar-Shalmaneser and made it a provincial centre; its Assyrian palace had notable wall paintings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/til-barsip.jpg" alt="Til Barsip" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David George Hogarth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tell_Ahmar_Across_Euphrates._Accidents_of_an_antiquary%27s_life._1910.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Til Barsip, also written Til Barsib, lies at Tell Ahmar in the Aleppo Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> about 20 kilometres downstream from ancient Carchemish. The site was inhabited as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. An important city arose there in the Early Bronze Age III period and was completely destroyed in Early Bronze Age IV, but the most important settlement on the mound is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the city was the Neo-Hittite capital of Masuwari. A well-preserved stele inscribed in Luwian, known as the Ahmar/Qubbah stele, commemorates a campaign by its king Hamiyatas around 900 BCE, records the cult of Tarhunzas of the Army and names the first king of Masuwari as Hapatila, perhaps an old Hurrian name. Hamiyatas set up further Luwian hieroglyphic inscriptions, and the scholar Woudhuizen has suggested Semitic readings of both kings' names.</p><p>Til Barsip later became the capital of the Aramean state of Bît Adini. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> captured it from its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuni">Ahuni</a> and renamed it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>, though the old name stayed in use, as did the Luwian language. Its position at a crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> made it a leading centre of Assyrian rule in the region, while nearby Carchemish kept its own kings until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> conquered it in 717 BCE. Inscribed steles record how the 8th-century BCE Aramean king Bar Ga'yah, who may be the same man as the Assyrian governor Shamshi-ilu, made a treaty with the city of Arpad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>David George Hogarth first examined the mound and proposed its identification as Til Barsip, and Gertrude Bell visited and photographed inscriptions there in 1909. The French archaeologist François Thureau-Dangin excavated from 1929 to 1931, uncovering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> city and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> burial chamber rich in pottery. Guy Bunnens of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> led excavations from the late 1980s until 2010, finding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> carvings of high quality published in 1997; later work has been under the University of Liège.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Til Barsip” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Late Bronze Age collapse, <b>Til Barsip</b> at Tell Ahmar was the Luwian city of Masuwari.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-adini">Bit Adini</a></li></ul></section>
